# HTML Mockup Design (no external tool)

Authoritative knowledge for the `ux-agent` when the project's design integration is **html-mockup**. Generates standalone, browser-viewable HTML files per screen × form factor. No MCP, no external tool, no API. Every output lands under `docs/design/<EPIC-SEQ>/screens/`.

Use cases:
- Greenfield projects without a design tool committed yet — show layout/components/states early.
- Backend / API features that need a thin reference UI for the contract.
- Bridging ASCII-text mockups (now retired) and a proper design tool adoption later.

The ux-agent loads this skill and follows it step by step.

---

## Inputs

| Path | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `docs/features/<id>/REQUIREMENTS.md` § Screen inventory | Authoritative screen list |
| `docs/features/<id>/REQUIREMENTS.md` § Functional requirements | Per-screen content + states |
| `docs/design/schema.json` | Workspace tokens (`tokens.{color,spacing,typography}`) — used as CSS vars if present |
| `CLAUDE.md` + `<framework>-patterns/SKILL.md` per declared stack | Form-factor mapping, component naming conventions |

## Outputs

| File | Mutation |
|---|---|
| `docs/design/<EPIC-SEQ>/screens/<screen>-<form>.html` | **Created** — one file per screen × form factor |
| `docs/design/<EPIC-SEQ>/tokens.css` | **Created** — CSS custom properties (mirrors `schema.json tokens.*`) |
| `docs/features/<id>/DESIGN.md` | **Created / replaced** — links to each .html, components list, implementation notes |
| `docs/features/<id>/REQUIREMENTS.md` § Visual spec | One-line link to DESIGN.md |
| `docs/features/<id>/state.json` | `design = complete`, `design_artifact = "docs/features/<id>/DESIGN.md"`, `design_provider = "html-mockup"` |

`docs/design/schema.json` `tokens.*` arrays — read-only here (no extraction; html-mockup consumes them, doesn't produce them).

---

## Phase 1 — Form-factor mapping

Same rule as figma — requirements-first, **never invent device support the requirements don't state** (an undeclared responsive/mobile variant doubles design + validation cost per screen). Decide in order; first match wins:

1. **Team convention** — `<framework>-patterns § Design system + visual conventions` specifies form factors → use those.
2. **Requirements** — `REQUIREMENTS.md` / PRD / story (incl. NFRs) mention responsive, mobile, tablet, breakpoints, or device support → exactly the named form factors: `mobile` (375w), `tablet` (768w), `desktop` (1440w) as applicable.
3. **Stack's native surface, single form factor** — web stack → `desktop` (1440w) only; mobile stack → `mobile` (375w) only. Record in DESIGN.md: `Single form factor (<name>) — requirements state no responsive/device support. Extend via /arh-iterate-design <id> --feedback "add <form factor>".`
4. **No frontend / mobile stack declared** — skip; emit `[NEEDS CLARIFICATION]` asking which form factors to design.

---

## Phase 2 — Token CSS file

Build `docs/design/<EPIC-SEQ>/tokens.css` from `docs/design/schema.json tokens.*` if non-empty:

```css
:root {
  /* Color */
  --color-bg-primary: #FFFFFF;
  --color-text-primary: #111111;
  --color-border-subtle: #E5E7EB;

  /* Spacing */
  --spacing-8: 8px;
  --spacing-16: 16px;
  --spacing-24: 24px;

  /* Typography */
  --text-heading-size: 24px;
  --text-heading-line-height: 32px;
  --text-body-size: 14px;
  --text-body-line-height: 20px;
}
```

**Fallback when `schema.json tokens.*` arrays empty:** emit a placeholder token set and a `[NEEDS CLARIFICATION]` block in DESIGN.md asking the team to populate the workspace tokens. The mockups remain valid (placeholder values), but downstream `/arh-implement` reads `[NEEDS CLARIFICATION]` and surfaces the gap.

---

## Phase 3 — Screen enumeration

```
For each screen S in REQUIREMENTS.md § Screen inventory:
  For each form factor F from Phase 1:
    emit one HTML file at:
      docs/design/<EPIC-SEQ>/screens/<S-slug>-<F>.html
```

`<S-slug>` is the lowercase, hyphen-separated screen name (e.g. `Bookmark List` → `bookmark-list`).

---

## Phase 4 — HTML template

Each generated file follows this exact shape (token vars consumed via CSS, components rendered as semantic HTML with `data-component` attributes):

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
  <title><Screen name> — <Form factor> — <EPIC-SEQ></title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="../tokens.css">
  <style>
    /* Form-factor frame */
    body { margin: 0; background: #F3F4F6; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; }
    .frame {
      width: <form-factor width>px;
      min-height: 100vh;
      margin: 0 auto;
      background: var(--color-bg-primary, #FFFFFF);
      color: var(--color-text-primary, #111111);
      padding: var(--spacing-16, 16px);
      box-sizing: border-box;
    }
    /* Lo-fi component shapes — outlines only; engineers replace with real components */
    [data-component] {
      outline: 1px dashed var(--color-border-subtle, #E5E7EB);
      padding: var(--spacing-8, 8px);
      margin-bottom: var(--spacing-8, 8px);
      border-radius: 4px;
    }
    [data-component]::before {
      content: attr(data-component);
      display: block;
      font-size: 10px;
      color: #6B7280;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.05em;
      margin-bottom: 4px;
    }
    [data-state="loading"] { opacity: 0.5; }
    [data-state="empty"]   { background: #F9FAFB; }
    [data-state="error"]   { border: 1px solid #DC2626; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="frame">
    <!-- One section per screen region, named via data-component -->
    <header data-component="Header">
      <h1><Screen title></h1>
    </header>

    <main>
      <!-- Populated state by default -->
      <section data-component="<ComponentName>">
        <!-- Representative content (NOT lorem ipsum) -->
      </section>

      <!-- Loading state stub -->
      <section data-component="<ComponentName>" data-state="loading" hidden>
        Loading…
      </section>

      <!-- Empty state stub -->
      <section data-component="<ComponentName>" data-state="empty" hidden>
        No items yet.
      </section>

      <!-- Error state stub -->
      <section data-component="<ComponentName>" data-state="error" hidden>
        Could not load.
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
```

### Rules

- **No raw colors / sizes in inline styles** — always reference `var(--color-*)` / `var(--spacing-*)` / `var(--text-*)`. Fallbacks (`var(--x, #FFF)`) are allowed for placeholder tokens.
- **One screen per file** — never combine multiple screens into one HTML page.
- **Component naming** — `data-component="<PascalCase name>"` matches the component vocabulary used in `<framework>-patterns`. The component shapes are intentionally lo-fi (dashed outline + label) — engineers replace with real components during `/arh-implement`.
- **All four states** (populated default, loading, empty, error) are present in every screen file, even if hidden. This forces designers to think about each state at draft time. Engineers can toggle `hidden` attributes for review.
- **Representative content** — use realistic domain strings (`$1,247.99` for a payments feature, `john.doe@example.com` for auth). Never `lorem ipsum`.

